        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is the ability of microbes to grow in the presence of antimicrobial agents that would normally kill them or inhibit their growth. Multidrugresistant (MDR) infections are those that are resistant to more than one class of antimicrobials.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "AMR can develop in several ways:",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Use of antimicrobials creates selection pressure, allowing growth of bacteria resistant to a specific drug by decreasing competition with susceptible organisms.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Antimicrobial-resistant bacteria can share genetic elements, conferring resistance to other bacteria.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Spontaneous new mutations can develop that allow bacterial survival in the face of antimicrobials.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "MDR infections occur through any of these mechanisms; resistance is often additive with antimicrobial use over time.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Any tissue affected by bacterial infections may be affected by antimicrobial-resistant bacterial infections. Some examples are:",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Renal/urologic—recurrent urinary tract infections.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Skin—abscesses, pyoderma.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Respiratory—pneumonia.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Musculoskeletal—surgical site infections.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Gastrointestinal—gastrointestinal pathogens such asCampylobacterspp.,Salmonellaspp., Escherichia coli,Enterococcusspp.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hepatobiliary—ascending cholangitis/cholecystitis.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cardiovascular—endocarditis.",
              "pdf_page": 991,
              "printed_page": 909,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
